WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.782
This slide contains three practice examples.

00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:04.912
Let's start with the generator.

00:00:04.912 --> 00:00:10.692
The squares function receives n and yields x squared for each value in the range.

00:00:10.692 --> 00:00:16.587
For this small example, we convert the generator to a list so we can easily see all the

00:00:16.587 --> 00:00:21.467
results. Remember that the generator itself normally produces the values one at a time.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.492
Next, we have the safe_divide function.

00:00:24.492 --> 00:00:27.607
This example is really about defensive programming.

00:00:27.607 --> 00:00:31.112
Before dividing, we check whether b is zero.

00:00:31.112 --> 00:00:36.907
If it is zero, we return a message instead of allowing Python to perform an invalid division.

00:00:36.907 --> 00:00:39.997
Run the example and then change zero to two.

00:00:39.997 --> 00:00:42.397
Notice how the behavior changes.

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:47.017
Finally, we compare a manual loop with Python's sum function.

00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:52.527
The manual approach starts with total equal to zero and adds each value individually.

00:00:52.527 --> 00:00:55.612
Then we calculate the same result using sum.

00:00:55.612 --> 00:00:58.067
Both answers should be identical.

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:02.877
This is a useful exercise because it shows that understanding the longer version helps us appreciate what the

00:01:02.877 --> 00:01:06.222
built-in function is doing for us.
